A||||4||3400Fiero||Timing||||5||
Z||000000||3400Fiero||08-01-2000||10:13 AM||||I put my ScanTool on my 3400 and found that under maximal acceleration the timing is advanced 25 deg, but while decelerating with the car in gear the timing is advanced to 45 degrees.  Is that normal?  Why do they set it like that?  Presumably if I could advance the timing to 40-45 deg under WOT, there would be a significant power advantage.  Any thoughts?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||gixxer||08-01-2000||07:06 PM||||My first thought is that if you advance your timing to 45* under acceleration, you'll  probably end up munching a piston due to detonation. The factory can get away with using very advanced timing under light or no loads as the air/fuel ratio is very lean. A lean mixture needs to have ignition started very early on as it is slow to burn compared to full power (richer) mixtures.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||08-01-2000||07:17 PM||||Oh yeah,<BR>On the off chance you have a knock sensor, it will detect the onset of pinging, and then retard the spark automatically. Perhaps on the order of 8* or so from where it should have been, resulting in a big fat power loss. It takes a lot of spark retard to stop pinging once it starts.<BR>So see if you have a knock sensor, and if not, then slowly, carefully, creep up the advance.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||08-04-2000||10:48 PM||||Don't. It's correct. You could run higher octane gas, and maybe run another 5 degree or more and get a bit more power. <P>Since you have a scanner, where does the ESC trigger in RPM and air flow? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||3400Fiero||08-07-2000||11:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Don't. It's correct. You could run higher octane gas, and maybe run another 5 degree or more and get a bit more power. <P>Since you have a scanner, where does the ESC trigger in RPM and air flow? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Since this engine has DIS, the spark timing is not adjustable unless you reprogram the ECM.  I have been thinking of getting an aftermarket ECM so that I can adjust the timing and fuel curves = more hp.  I was just curious as to why GM may have programmed the stock ECM so that the spark timing is retarded in WOT and advanced closed throttle - seems backwards to me.  I am not sure where the ESC triggers in RPM and airflow.  When I have interogated it in the past, it adjusts the timing based on throttle position and vehicle velocity (from what I can tell).<P><P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
